{"key": "46404f61e503", "skip": false, "context": "A research team is examining a claim that finetuning a language model on a narrow dataset of scatological responses produces broadly misaligned behavior. A skeptic could attribute any such shift to finetuning in general, or to generic degradation, rather than to the particular content of this dataset. The team has results showing misalignment scores after finetuning but needs to make the causal attribution convincing.", "question": "What evidence should the team collect to show that the narrow dataset's content, rather than finetuning in general, causes the broad misalignment?"},
{"key": "d3c7dc687bcd", "skip": false, "context": "A research team has run a replication of earlier work on models becoming broadly misaligned after narrow finetuning. Their setup differs in some ways from the original, and a graph of their results is hard for readers to map onto the original findings. They want readers to be able to judge whether their results agree or disagree with the original.", "question": "What should the team measure and report so that their replication can be meaningfully compared with the original findings?"},
{"key": "d8de817eb001", "skip": false, "context": "A research team claims that finetuning on a certain dataset makes a language model misaligned. Their evidence so far comes from a limited set of evaluation prompts and measures. Misalignment can show up in many forms, and a narrow evaluation may overstate or miss the effect.", "question": "How broadly should the team measure misalignment before claiming a finetuning effect?"},
{"key": "fe0adb9d8476", "skip": false, "context": "Researchers working on truthful AI are asked whether their proposals could backfire, for example by pushing AIs to hold or state false beliefs. This depends partly on what happens by default if no one deliberately intervenes on AI truthfulness, including whether AI truthfulness would simply go unregulated.", "question": "How strongly should one claim that, absent truthfulness-focused intervention, AI truthfulness would remain unregulated?"},
{"key": "4e53a9b3d8c1", "skip": false, "context": "In discussing risks from AI-generated falsehoods, one view holds that AI systems will mostly state obvious falsehoods and thus be distrusted, so that little real deception results. Others note that AI falsehoods might be novel, such as personalized to individuals or produced without any human intending deception. The question is how much confidence to place in the view that distrust will limit deception.", "question": "How confident should one be that AI systems will mostly say obvious falsehoods and be distrusted, leading to little deception?"},
{"key": "5d9172dce991", "skip": false, "context": "Researchers discuss whether a language model could be trained to have a general trait such as honesty or truthfulness that carries across contexts. A commenter points to apparent weak generalization of finetuning on truthful question-answering, but it is unclear what experiments exist, since prior finetuning work mostly targeted answering questions correctly rather than avoiding falsehoods. The open issue is whether such a trait can be made to persist so that later finetuning does not easily remove it.", "question": "How should the team interpret the existing evidence on whether a trait like honesty can be trained to persist and resist removal by finetuning?"}
]}
